  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia invests in SPC&#x2019;s Educational Quality &amp; Assessment Program (EQAP), a regional public good. EQAP provides technical education services to 15 Pacific island countries (PICs) on student assessment, education information systems and using data to inform policies and planning. EQAP administers a Pacific Year 13 in Vanuatu, Kiribati and Tuvalu (SPFSC) as well as the Pacific Islands Literacy and Numeracy Assessment (PILNA) of 40,000 primary students in 15 PICs, which is the leading data source on primary education outcomes. EQAP presented the latest PILNA results to ministers at the Conference of Pacific Education Ministers (CPEM) in March 2023.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Education</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia supports the Educational Quality and Assessment Programme (EQAP) co-financed by New Zealand and implemented by the Pacific Community (SPC) with technical support from the Australian Council for Educational Research (ACER). EQAP is a regional public good that provides demand driven technical services and support to 15 Pacific island countries (PICs), including student assessments, curriculum development, and data informed policy and planning. Administered by EQAP every three years, the Pacific Islands Literacy and Numeracy Assessment (PILNA) is the Pacific&#x2019;s largest regional assessment of literacy and numeracy for students in Grade 4 and Grade 6. In 2024, EQAP conducted in-country PILNA field trials in all 15 PICs and piloted the Pacific Assessment for Lower Secondary (PALS) in 10 schools across 4 PICs.  It also supported quality assurance of national exams in Nauru, Samoa, Tuvalu and Vanuatu. In March 2025, at the Conference of Pacific Education Ministers (CPEM) in Papua New Guinea, EQAP launched the 2024 Status of Pacific Education Report (SOPER) which contributes to global education reporting (including SDG reporting) alongside PILNA regional and country reports.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Education</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia supports the Pacific Community&#x2019;s Educational Quality Assessment Programme (EQAP). EQAP provides technical education services to 15 Pacific Island Countries (PICs) on student assessment, education information systems and using data to inform policies and planning.  Highlights for 2023 included: a data workshop with 12 PICs; the introduction of &#x2018;PILNA TV&#x2019; in Fiji (with online literacy intervention videos); and the piloting of a new assessment tool for lower secondary students.  EQAP released the 2022 Status of Pacific Education Report in 2023, which contributes to global education reporting (including SDG reporting).</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
